INT. SALT-WATER BATH HOUSE - BRIGHTON - 1832 - NIGHT

A cavernous Georgian room. A long rectangular plunge pool of dark seawater, steaming faintly. Round arched windows. A lamp on every column. Brass taps tarnished green. The smell of brine and old stone.

On a marble bench along the pool's edge, MRS. HEPZIBAH QUARLES-ODUYA (60s, black bombazine, a stick with a silver handle) sits very upright, a hat box on her lap.

At the far end, LORD PERCIVAL ABERCROMBIE-ASANTE (30s, dressing gown, wet hair, a flush of fever) leans against a column, arms folded.

Between them, in the water up to her waist, SUKI ADEYEMI-FAIRBANKS (20s, bathing costume of striped flannel, red from the cold, a determined jaw) wades with her chin lifted.

HEPZIBAH
That is quite enough, Susannah.

SUKI
It's Suki.

HEPZIBAH
It is Susannah on the registry.

SUKI
The registry is wrong.

LORD PERCIVAL
(from the column)
Rather a spirited young woman.

HEPZIBAH
Rather an impertinent one.

SUKI
(to the pool)
I only wanted a swim.

HEPZIBAH
At midnight.

SUKI
At midnight no one stares.

LORD PERCIVAL
I am staring.

SUKI
Then you are no one.

He laughs, a short bark. Hepzibah turns her stick in her hands.

HEPZIBAH
Lord Percival, I trust you will not repeat this.

LORD PERCIVAL
Madam, I shall repeat it at every dinner for a year.

HEPZIBAH
I beg you will not.

SUKI
Let him, Aunt.

HEPZIBAH
I will not be talked of.

SUKI
You already are.

A long silence. Lamplight trembles on the black water. Suki ducks under, rises, shakes her hair.
